= Distributed cognition =

== Overview ==
'''Distributed cognition''' is an approach that studies cognitive processes at the level of systems containing interacting people, artifacts, representations, and environments.

Instead of assuming that the individual brain is always the complete unit of analysis, distributed cognition asks how information is represented, transformed, coordinated, and propagated across a larger functional system.

== Unit of analysis ==
A distributed-cognition analysis can include:

* people with different roles;
* written or digital representations;
* instruments and interfaces;
* procedures;
* physical arrangement;
* communication channels;
* temporal coordination.

The boundary is chosen according to the functional cognitive system being studied.

== Example ==
Navigation on a ship can depend on several crew members, charts, instruments, procedures, and communicated measurements.

No one person needs to internally contain the entire computation performed by the coordinated system.

This does not imply that the group has a mysterious independent mind.

== External representations ==
Artifacts can change the difficulty and structure of cognitive work.

A checklist, diagram, map, calculator, or interface can store state, constrain options, expose relationships, and reduce internal [[Working memory|working-memory]] demands.

== Distributed cognition and cognitive offloading ==
Using an external tool can be a form of cognitive offloading.

Distributed cognition goes further by analyzing how internal and external representations participate together in a coordinated cognitive process.

== Distributed cognition and distributed computing ==
Distributed computing concerns computation across networked machines.

Distributed cognition is a cognitive-science framework about functional cognitive systems and can include humans and artifacts.

The terms are not synonyms.

== Related concepts ==
[[Human-computer interaction]], [[Distributed system]], [[System boundary]], and [[Knowledge representation]] provide important neighboring concepts while remaining distinct from distributed cognition itself.

